1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,852
Rivers carry contamination downstream faster than any lab result.

2
00:00:04,852 --> 00:00:08,199
PathoStream EHR spots it, forecasts where it goes,

3
00:00:08,199 --> 00:00:11,412
and warns the doctor before the patients arrive,

4
00:00:11,412 --> 00:00:14,492
using the health standards FHIR and CDS Hooks.

5
00:00:17,860 --> 00:00:20,482
This is the water authority dashboard:

6
00:00:20,482 --> 00:00:23,656
nine real European rivers, forty-one stations,

7
00:00:23,656 --> 00:00:27,244
fifteen countries, and one pipeline for every river.

8
00:00:28,260 --> 00:00:30,694
On German rivers, this badge is live,

9
00:00:30,694 --> 00:00:35,892
real water-level data, kept separate from our simulated contamination readings.

10
00:00:37,020 --> 00:00:41,522
Path one: a water official confirms contamination at Ponte de Santa

11
00:00:41,522 --> 00:00:42,732
Clara, in Coimbra.

12
00:00:43,740 --> 00:00:47,908
A transport model forecasts when it reaches each station downstream,

13
00:00:47,908 --> 00:00:50,851
with a time range, not one falsely exact number.

14
00:00:50,851 --> 00:00:53,364
We move the clock forward twenty minutes.

15
00:00:54,380 --> 00:00:55,770
Now the doctor's screen.

16
00:00:55,770 --> 00:00:59,535
A patient living nearby gets an alert inside their health record.

17
00:00:59,535 --> 00:01:01,794
It starts with one line in plain words,

18
00:01:01,794 --> 00:01:04,748
then a suggested stool test with its verified code.

19
00:01:05,740 --> 00:01:08,036
Path two: nobody reports anything.

20
00:01:08,036 --> 00:01:10,130
The water slowly gets cloudier.

21
00:01:10,130 --> 00:01:12,561
A trend check watches every station,

22
00:01:12,561 --> 00:01:15,262
and after six unusual readings in a row,

23
00:01:15,262 --> 00:01:17,356
it flags the station by itself.

24
00:01:19,300 --> 00:01:22,883
Same patient, weaker evidence, so a different alert:

25
00:01:22,883 --> 00:01:27,156
amber, clearly marked not confirmed, with no treatment advice.

26
00:01:27,156 --> 00:01:30,532
The alert is only ever as strong as the evidence.

27
00:01:31,460 --> 00:01:35,252
Path three: a member of the public reports cloudy water,

28
00:01:35,252 --> 00:01:37,013
a bad smell and dead fish,

29
00:01:37,013 --> 00:01:39,112
a warning sign for animals too.

30
00:01:39,112 --> 00:01:42,092
An AI model scores the report and shows why.

31
00:01:44,100 --> 00:01:46,232
But the AI never confirms anything.

32
00:01:46,232 --> 00:01:48,972
Only a water official can approve the report.

33
00:01:48,972 --> 00:01:51,469
Then the doctor's alert is full strength,

34
00:01:51,469 --> 00:01:53,844
and it names the citizen as the source.

35
00:01:55,140 --> 00:01:59,564
That report is exported as FHIR in the OneAquaHealth guide's own

36
00:01:59,564 --> 00:02:02,951
format, and the official HL7 validator checks it:

37
00:02:02,951 --> 00:02:03,780
zero errors.

38
00:02:04,620 --> 00:02:07,090
Every event lands on one timeline:

39
00:02:07,090 --> 00:02:09,924
the audit trail across all nine rivers.

40
00:02:11,100 --> 00:02:12,149
Now the AI layer.

41
00:02:12,149 --> 00:02:14,308
Four agents look at the river data,

42
00:02:14,308 --> 00:02:17,886
the citizen reports, the patient, and the overall picture.

43
00:02:17,886 --> 00:02:22,020
Google Gemini chooses what to check, and a guard checks every call.

44
00:02:23,100 --> 00:02:25,434
The evidence is confirmed by a person,

45
00:02:25,434 --> 00:02:29,057
so the commander suggests closing the river for recreation,

46
00:02:29,057 --> 00:02:30,286
but only as a draft.

47
00:02:30,286 --> 00:02:32,988
Patient data stays on our server by default.

48
00:02:34,020 --> 00:02:36,888
Gemini writes these summaries, clearly labelled.

49
00:02:36,888 --> 00:02:39,636
They can't change a single number or decision.

50
00:02:40,660 --> 00:02:43,398
Every notice is a draft, marked not issued.

51
00:02:43,398 --> 00:02:44,990
A person must approve it.

52
00:02:44,990 --> 00:02:46,900
Nothing is sent automatically.

53
00:02:47,940 --> 00:02:51,119
Every step is traceable: what Gemini asked for,

54
00:02:51,119 --> 00:02:53,892
what ran, and any call the guard blocked.

55
00:02:54,860 --> 00:02:58,022
Now the contrast: the confirmed flag is cleared,

56
00:02:58,022 --> 00:03:01,052
leaving only an automatic, unconfirmed signal.

57
00:03:02,300 --> 00:03:06,212
We run the same agents again, for a patient with bloody diarrhea.

58
00:03:08,260 --> 00:03:12,052
With only an unconfirmed signal, the one thing the system may

59
00:03:12,052 --> 00:03:14,103
suggest is taking a water sample.

60
00:03:14,103 --> 00:03:15,844
Never a boil-water advisory.

61
00:03:16,740 --> 00:03:20,313
Clinically, disease scores are labelled as rough rankings,

62
00:03:20,313 --> 00:03:23,886
not probabilities, and only verified test codes are shown.

63
00:03:23,886 --> 00:03:25,980
The review priority stays routine.

64
00:03:26,980 --> 00:03:30,076
And nothing is guessed: bad input is refused.

65
00:03:31,220 --> 00:03:34,675
The Gemini safeguards: a fixed tool list for each agent,

66
00:03:34,675 --> 00:03:38,623
scope locked to the request, patient details kept on our server,

67
00:03:38,623 --> 00:03:40,412
and limits on calls and time.

68
00:03:41,540 --> 00:03:45,485
PathoStream EHR: warn the doctor before the patients arrive.

69
00:03:45,485 --> 00:03:48,050
A research prototype on simulated data,

70
00:03:48,050 --> 00:03:51,666
not a medical device, with a fourteen-risk safety file,

71
00:03:51,666 --> 00:03:53,770
and every safety control tested.

72
00:03:53,770 --> 00:03:54,428
Thank you.

